From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 02:53:26 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] The edition's stereo 3D view: fitted to the person's
 depth limits

Sparky Stereo OS builds this on the optional stereo preview (pull
request 7112) for its own package:

- The orthographic eyes are parallel views of one world: the projection
  is sheared about the point looked at, x' = x + s (d - dist), instead of
  turning the eye, which is toe-in.
- The separation is the person's depth limits, set once in the desktop's
  3D settings and read through libstereo-declare in the pixels of the
  view's buffer (Wayland: the top-level window's surface; X11: the view's
  own stereo window), four times a second, so a change redraws an open
  view.  Before the left eye the view is drawn once from the camera
  between the eyes and its depth is read after the scene, before the 2D
  items over it; the separation is the largest for which the nearest and
  the farthest pixel stay inside the limits.  The eye separation setting
  is gone.
- "Enable 3D" is on by default and asks for stereo buffers in the 3D
  view's own format only, so the rest of the window stays one picture.
  A button opens the desktop's Display Settings.
- A double click reads the depth of the view drawn from the camera
  between the eyes, so it finds the point a single view finds.
- What cannot be shown in 3D is said once in the console: no limits, the
  limits not delivered after two seconds or not readable, the depth not
  readable, or a view without stereo buffers on a desktop that offers
  them.  A person who enables 3D where the desktop offers no stereo
  buffers is told in a dialog.
- QT_LOGGING_RULES="openscad.stereo.debug=true" prints each frame's fit.

